$ do phase("night", 3)
It is the first night in the house, and it is cold enough that the water in the basin has a skin on it, and there are, by your own reckoning, four things in it that want doing and time for three.
-> night

# road
## Three weeks
The berline has its blinds sewn shut, and a brazier at the foot of the seat that is for the horses' sake and not for yours, and it goes up through the Piedmont and over the pass in a sleet that makes the postilions sing. You are not very good at being carried. You lie in the dark and listen to the horses, and to the wheels, and to the man on the box with a flask, and you count the weeks in post houses, in each of which a boy comes out with a lantern and looks at the number plate and does not look in.
?if wake("steward") == "keepers"
  Étienne sits across from you for the whole of it, with a rug over his knees and a small book in his lap, and reads, by a lantern with a hood, in the intervals when it is safe. He reads about the French in the way that a man reads about his own relations. On the second week, on the far side of the mountains, at three in the morning, he puts the book down and says, "Monsieur, I should like to know something, and I think that I am afraid to ask." You tell him to ask. He asks whether it is true that you were there when the Great Fire was lit. You say that it is. He says, "Ah." After a long time he says that it is a great deal to know that one's family has been looking at one for seventeen centuries, and that he hopes that he has not embarrassed them.
?elif wake("steward") == "fiammetta"
  A boy from the hill rides on the box, one of the charcoal burners' sons, lent by Fiammetta, for the sake of the air and a pistol. He comes in at the halts with his teeth chattering and sits for a quarter of an hour in the corner telling you what he can about his employer, which is that she does not sleep, and that she has never once raised her voice, and that nobody on the hill has ever been quite able to leave. He has a note from her in his hat. It says, *The family in Paris are cousins. They are to be told what they need and not what they want.*
?else
  Anselme rides on the box, for the sake of the air, and comes in at the halts, with his teeth chattering, and sits for a quarter of an hour in the corner telling you what he can about the house in the rue Vivienne and the state of the roads. He says, in a voice from which he has plainly tried to remove anything that could be written down, that the Valères are a very good family. He says that they are a printing family. He says that the shop is not, so far as he can tell, quite what the police think it is, and that he is sure that it is quite what the family thinks.
?end
There is a very long night at Lyon, in an inn with a sagging bed and a great number of cats, and then Burgundy, in frost, with the vines standing in black rows in the snow like the staves of a cage, and then the plain, and the poplars, and the long white road to Paris.
<~

# road_hunger
## The hunger on the road
The hunger comes on you somewhere between Dijon and Sens. It is not a surprise. You have been walking a long time on the edge of it, and in the corner of the berline in the dark, with the post horses steaming and a young postilion alone in the yard with a bucket, it comes in like a flood.
* Take the postilion, quickly, and leave him sleeping in the straw
  $ do feed("human")
  $ humanity -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  It is very quick, and very cold, and he does not wake until the horses have been changed. He will say in the morning that he fell asleep in the straw, and nobody will think it odd, because it is the third week of a very bad winter and every postilion in France has fallen asleep in a manger. It is the sort of crime that nobody has ever remarked upon. That is no comfort.
* Ask for a horse, and take what you need from the stable
  $ do feed("animal")
  $ humanity += 1
  The post master is very much surprised to be woken, and is sure that it is the strangest thing that anyone has ever asked him. He is paid enough that he does not ask why. The horse is a patient bay with a white sock, and it stands through all of it with the resigned eyes of an animal who has been asked to give blood to very much stranger things than you.
* Wait. Take nothing, and endure it to the next stage
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ humanity += 1
  It is a long hundred miles. You sit with your face in the corner and a handkerchief over your mouth, and you count the posts. You do not scream. It is a very near thing at Fontainebleau, where a girl with a basket of hot chestnuts leans in at the window to sell you one, and you take her hand to pay, and hold it for a moment too long, and let it go.
<~

# arrival
## The wall
You come into Paris in the dark, on the second of December, by the road from the south, and the first that you know of it is a light, and a rope across the road, and a fat man in a cocked hat who says that he is a servant of the Farmers General and wants to know what you carry.
It is a toll house. It is a good deal larger than a toll house ought to be. It is of stone, with columns, in the manner of a Greek temple that has been asked to collect money, and it is one of fifty that stand in a ring round the whole of the city, joined by a wall, that has been built in the last four years by the tax farmers to catch the wine and the salt and the firewood on their way in. Nobody likes it. The people say that the wall that walls Paris makes Paris murmur.
The man in the hat is not interested in you. He is interested in the boxes, and he prods the straw with a long rod, and finds nothing of any consequence, and a bottle of brandy, which he keeps. You are let through, and the berline goes on, over the frozen ruts, into the dark.
Paris smells of woodsmoke and wet horse and frozen mud. It is the most crowded place that you have ever been, and it is, in the black of December, almost entirely silent. There are lanterns, hung on ropes across the streets, that sway in the wind, and under each of them a little puddle of yellow light with a skin of ice on it. The houses go up on each side to five and six stories, each leaning a little toward its neighbor, as though for warmth. In the doorways there are people, a good many people, sitting with their knees up and their hands in their sleeves, who do not look up as you go by. They are waiting for something. It takes you a long time to understand that it is morning, and the bakers.
The rue Saint-Jacques runs from the river to the wall in a long straight spine of stone, up the hill, past the colleges, past the Sorbonne with its great gray dome like a head with a hat, and it is full of printing. There are shops with presses in the windows, and shops with bound books in the windows, and a shop with a sign of a golden sun, rubbed nearly bare by weather, that says in a very small letter underneath that it is the Soleil d'Or. You look at it for a good while. It is a good deal less presumptuous than the king's.
$ do chart("paris.valere")
$ do discover("the_barrieres")
<~

# greeting
## The door
It is a tall narrow house, of gray stone, with a door in the middle and a shop on the ground floor with the shutters up and a lamp over the lintel. There is a smell of ink and warm paper and lamp oil, which is a very different smell from the one that you remember from the last century's scriptoria. There is a boy of nine with a broom. There is a yard at the back, and a stair, and a cellar that goes a great deal further down than the house is high.
?if _etienne_new == 0
  It is Étienne's own door, and he opens it with his own key, with the dazed relief of a man who has been three weeks away from home and had not entirely expected to return. There are a great many people in the passage, and a smell of bread, and a clatter of dishes, and a general sense of a household that has been tidied for an inspection.
?else
  The door is opened from inside before you have lifted a hand. A lean gray man of sixty, in a printer's apron over a good black coat, stands in the passage with a candle. He has Aelia's chin. You have seen that chin on a hundred faces in a thousand years. He looks at you for a very long moment, and then at the candle, as if he suspected that it were lying, and says, in the careful voice of a man who has practiced it, "Monsieur Colline. The house of Valère. The house is honored."
?end
Behind him, in the passage, there is a young woman with her arms folded and a pen behind her ear, who has plainly decided, on a number of previous occasions, that she is not going to be impressed. There is a lad of twenty, in an ink-stained shirt, with a great deal of black hair and the beginnings of a mustache, who is gazing at you as if you had been delivered from the sky. And there is a lean tall man of forty, in a faded blue coat with the facings picked off, leaning on the newel post with a pipe, who looks at you with an expression of the most complete and friendly interest, and says nothing at all.
You are aware that the next words that you say will be repeated.
:the_title
* Bow low and say "Monseigneur", as the manners of your last century require
  $ b3.titles = "monseigneur"
  $ standing -= 1
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  Étienne goes red to the roots of his hair. Behind him the young woman puts her hand over her mouth. The boy with the broom says "Monseigneur" back, with great feeling, and is cuffed. "Monsieur," says Étienne, in agony, "I am a printer. In this street the last man who was called Monseigneur was a bishop, and we set his sermon in italics." The man at the newel post takes the pipe from his mouth. "In America," he says, with relish, "it would be treason." You have the very distinct feeling that you have just been added to the household as a source of anecdote.
* Say "Monsieur", and watch what the others do
  $ b3.titles = "monsieur"
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  It is the safest word in the language, and it is plainly a good one. Étienne lets out his breath. The young woman unfolds her arms by a quarter of an inch. You have the sense of having crossed some very large room on tiptoe and of having been seen to do it, and, on the whole, approved.
* Ask whether a title is required, and be told, with relief, that you may be a monsieur
  $ b3.titles = "asked"
  $ humanity += 1
  "Required?" says Étienne. "Monsieur, in the rue Saint-Jacques we have not required a title of anyone since the Fronde." The man at the newel post says that in his experience, which is a long one and mostly of military kitchens, the less a man is called the more he is liked. The young woman says, flatly, that it is the same with money. It is the first thing that she has said, and it is, you find, very much to the point.
They are brought forward and named. The young woman is Adélaïde, Étienne's daughter, a widow at twenty nine, who keeps the books and the keys and the temper of the household. The lad is Baptiste, her brother, a compositor, who has set in type every pamphlet that was ever printed in the Latin quarter and has opinions on most of them. The man at the post is Captain Aubry, who has the third floor and an excellent view, a half-pay officer of Rochambeau's army, lately returned from Virginia. He says that he has been told that you are a Florentine, and a patron of the house, and that you are not to be asked how old you are. He says that this is the best news that he has heard since Yorktown.
$ do meet("adelaide")
$ do meet("baptiste")
$ do meet("aubry")
$ do kin("adelaide")
$ do kin("baptiste")
